{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"=== SESSION: e369 Jira DC — every child closed, 4 harness rounds, 6 -> 3 live failures ===\n\n--- OUTCOME ---\nALL 15 children closed: the 8 stories (J1-J8) were already closed; the 7 open bugs\n(3fe5, 1a9f, 37e7, 39c1, 9bb9, 18a5, 5200) closed this session, plus 339a, discovered\nand fixed within it. The epic's own 13 ACs are under independent verification.\n\n--- THE HARNESS ARC, one pinned DC 8.17.1 image ---\n  30857265932  collected=58   6 failed / 51 passed / 1 xfailed   (baseline)\n  30867072561  collected=58   5 failed / 52 passed / 1 xfailed\n  30874380112  collected=61   4 failed / 56 passed / 1 xfailed\n  30880394880  collected=61   3 failed / 57 passed / 1 xfailed   (final)\n\nCOVERAGE ROSE 58 -> 61 WHILE FAILURES FELL 6 -> 3, with `skipped` pinned at exactly 1\n(the canary) in every run. The direction of those two numbers together is the claim;\neither alone is gameable.\n\nThe 3 remaining reds are ONE measured platform limitation — DC will not move or clear a\nSUB-TASK's `fields.parent` — recorded in docs/jira-dc-capability-map.md with evidence ids.\n\n--- MERGED (14) ---\n  1330 37e7 clear Epic Link, not sub-task parent      1341 39c1 pass-level epic parent cell\n  1331 465d transport split 799 -> 230, five mixins   1342 9bb9 epic-parent idempotence cell\n  1332 3fe5 pin the harness project template          1343 339a stop re-pushing a removed parent\n  1333 259b record what the pinned DC image does      1345 18a5 shape priority and assignee\n  1337 1a9f verify the sub-task parent write          1346 1a9f assert the refusal live\n  1340 18a5 translate the create payload              1348 18a5 drop null optionals, wrap parent\n\n--- 339a: A DEAD FEATURE WITH A GREEN SUITE ---\n88d9's inbound parent-clear was UNREACHABLE in any bidirectional pass. The outbound differ\nre-pushed the parent in the same pass and inbound discarded its own clear as a contradiction;\nthe clear's precondition and the re-push's precondition are IDENTICAL, so it could never fire.\n\nWhy the 88d9 suite stayed green: every cell passes `outbound_mutations=None`, and the ONE cell\nsupplying outbound context HAND-BUILDS the entry and asserts the suppression as desired. That is\na real oracle asserting the wrong thing because its input was fabricated — a step beyond a\nvacuous double, and the more dangerous shape.\n\nFound only because 37e7's rewrite finally reached its precondition and failed somewhere new.\n\n--- 18a5: THREE LAYERS, EACH HIDDEN BY THE ONE ABOVE ---\nA single 400 aborts the whole create, so each fix revealed the next:\n  1. bridge-schema NAMES  ticket_type/title/status posted as Jira field ids\n  2. value SHAPES         priority and assignee sent bare; Jira REST wants objects\n  3. NULLS + parent key   assignee arrives as None (drop it); parent wraps {\"key\":…} not {\"name\":…}\nSeventh instance of \"Cloud has the translation, DC never got its half\" — and the first where\nthe seam was missed for names AND shapes AND nulls.\n\n--- METHOD, THE PART WORTH CARRYING ---\n1. I CLOSED 39c1 ON THE WRONG EVIDENCE and reopened it. AC1 demanded a PASS-LEVEL assertion;\n   I ticked it from a cell that calls `set_parent` directly. Hours earlier I had refused to tick\n   that same AC from the capability map for exactly that reason, then collapsed the distinction\n   one step later. Adjacent-green substitution is not a thing you learn once.\n2. HELD-OUT TDD KEEPS PAYING. On 3fe5 and 18a5 the held-out cells caught what the visible ones\n   could not. On 1a9f the implementer resolved a conflict IN PRODUCTION CODE by treating an\n   absent read-back field as \"no evidence\" — defensible in the abstract, but it existed only to\n   satisfy a double that does not model state. Making the doubles faithful instead exposed a\n   SECOND stale double the first fix would have masked.\n3. A FALSIFIABLE PREDICTION THAT COULD NOT BE TESTED IS NOT A REFUTATION. The 339a trace predicted\n   a `RECON: outbound_update` breadcrumb. It was absent from the CI log — and so were ALL `RECON:`\n   lines, because the harness captures pass stderr into `cp.stderr` and prints it only on\n   assertion. Inconclusive, not negative. The local test then produced it directly.\n4. TWO SUBAGENTS REPORTED CONTRADICTIONS RATHER THAN MASSAGING THEM: one that its brief predicted\n   the wrong casualty, one that the mechanism refuted its own starting hypothesis. Both were right.\n\n--- INFRASTRUCTURE FOUND ALONG THE WAY ---\n* [rebar:94d0-5d19-2d06-4fa7] LIVE CREDENTIALS in a ticket COMMENT (129e) blocked every\n  tickets-branch push for ~8h, stranding 122 commits. Push protection held — the secrets never\n  reached GitHub — but the store reported success to every writer while diverging from the\n  remote. The silent auto-push failure is arguably the worse defect. Operator resolved.\n* A review-bot maintenance window produced a fail-closed LLM-Review -1 whose signature is\n  indistinguishable from a permissions regression; I filed it as a defect and the operator\n  corrected me. Recorded because the inference was well-formed and still wrong.\n* `d2ce: default local dev to Bedrock` landed mid-session, so the completion verifier began\n  resolving to Bedrock and closes failed until `pydantic-ai-slim[bedrock]` was installed —\n  fixed properly rather than bypassed with --force-close."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785825944255438001,"uuid":"ae5e05c7-f057-414a-b8c1-b9879dcc2097"}